## Code Example
```c
void handle_write_fault(PTE *pte) {
  acquire(pte->lock);
  if (pte->refcount > 1) {
    Page *newpage = alloc_page();
    memcpy(newpage->addr, pte->frame->addr, PAGE_SIZE);
    pte->frame = newpage;
    pte->writable = 1;
    pte->refcount--;
    invalidate_tlb(pte->vaddr);
  } else {
    pte->writable = 1;
  }
  release(pte->lock);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of process lifecycle, waitpid usage, and zombie avoidance in a practical multi-child scenario.

## Key Concepts
- waitpid semantics and looping
- SIGCHLD handling and reaping
- WNOHANG and non-blocking variants
- Zombie processes and edge cases (stopped/continued)

## Code Example
```javascript
#include <sys/types.h>
#include <sys/wait.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <errno.h>

int main() {
  // spawn N children ...
  int finished = 0; int status;
  while (finished < N) {
    pid_t pid = waitpid(-1, &status, 0);
    if (pid > 0) { finished++; /* process status */ }
    else if (pid == -1 && errno == ECHILD) break;
  }
  return 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for SIGCHLD storms or large N?
- What are the risks of ignoring zombie cleanup in long-running daemons?

Why This Is Asked
Tests lock-free data structures, cross-core coordination, and end-to-end IO flow. It gauges ability to design scalable, low-latency paths with correct completion routing.

Key Concepts
- Per-device queues and shared completion path
- Lock-free enqueue/dequeue via CAS
- Correct delivery to originating process
- Backpressure, burst handling, and deadlock avoidance

Code Example
```c
// Pseudo-C: per-device ring enqueue
typedef struct req { void* buf; size_t len; int op; } req_t;
typedef struct ring { _Atomic(int) head, tail; req_t* buf[N]; } ring_t;
int enqueue(ring_t* r, req_t* e){
  int t = atomic_load(&r->tail);
  int next = (t + 1) % N;
  if (next == atomic_load(&r->head)) return -1; // full
  r->buf[t] = e;
  atomic_store(&r->tail, next);
  return 0;
}
req_t* dequeue(ring_t* r){
  int h = atomic_load(&r->head);
  int t = atomic_load(&r->tail);
  if (h == t) return NULL; // empty
  req_t* e = r->buf[h];
  atomic_store(&r->head, (h + 1) % N);
  return e;
}
``` 

Follow-up Questions
- How to scale with multiple devices sharing completion rings?
- How to handle out-of-order completions and per-process cancellation?
- What tests validate fairness under mixed device load?

